## rooms.add
A random rectangle that comes within a tile of no room placed so far is carved as a room [[c01:22-46 g]]. It is room {n}.

## rooms.discard
This rectangle comes within a tile of a room already placed, so it is thrown away [[c01:22-53 t:54-55]]. Rooms placed so far: {n}.

## rooms.sort
The rooms are sorted by their left edge, the order they will be joined in [[c01:57-69]]. Rooms to join: {n}.

## rooms.corridor
A corridor joins this room’s center to the center of the room before it: one run across and one up or down, in an order a coin flip picks [[c01:67-78 c01:85-101 t:57-58 g]]. It is corridor {n}.

## bsp.subdivide
The demo starts its list with one rectangle inside the map’s edge and that rectangle’s four quarters [[c02:24-29 c02:87-111]]. Quarters shown so far: {n}.

## bsp.add
An attempt picks any rectangle on the list and tries a room near its top-left corner [[c02:43-44 c02:114-136]]. The room is carved only if every tile within two of it is still solid and inside the map’s outer ring, and then the rectangle it came from is quartered again [[c02:45-52 c02:138-174 g]]. It is room {n}.

## cellular.noise
Every tile, the map’s edge included, starts as wall with a {n}% chance and as floor otherwise [[c03:19-29 g]].

## cellular.iterate
Every tile inside the map’s outer ring counts the walls among its eight neighbors, reading a copy of the map made before the pass [[c03:40-70 t:79-80 g]]. With five or more, or none, it becomes wall; with one to four, floor [[c03:61-66 t:81-82]]. This is pass {n}.

## drunkard.solid
The map starts solid [[c04:17-19 t:87 g]].

## drunkard.dig
A digger steps in a random direction and digs the tile it lands on, again and again, until it steps off the map or runs out of steps [[c04:54-76 t:90-95 g]]. The first starts at the center and each later one on a random open tile [[c04:21-22 c04:25-47 t:95-96 g]]. This is digger {n}.

## dla.seed
A plus of five open tiles at the center of a solid map: the seed that everything else sticks to [[c05:19-40 t:103-104 g]].

## dla.particle
A particle starts on a random tile and steps at random until it steps onto open floor. The solid tile it stepped from is dug [[c05:49-82 t:104-108 g]]. This is particle {n}.

## dla.particle#c06-dla-attractor
A particle starts on a random tile and travels in a straight line toward the center. The last solid tile it stood on before open floor is dug [[c06:49-68 t:111-112 g]]. This is particle {n}.

## dla.particle#c07-dla-symmetry
A particle travels straight toward the center, and the tile it digs is dug again at its mirror image across the map’s middle column [[c07:49-77 t:115-116 g]]. This is particle {n}.

## dla-erode.start
Erosion starts from a finished map: in the talk’s demo, random rooms joined by corridors [[c08:21-83 t:191-192]].

## dla-erode.particle
A particle starts on a random open tile and steps at random until it stands on rock. That one tile of rock is dug [[c08:86-128 t:192-193 g]]. This is particle {n}.

## voronoi.seeds
Seed points are scattered at random inside the map’s outer ring [[c08a:21-26 t:121-122]]. Seeds: {n}.

## voronoi.membership.pythagoras
Every tile joins its nearest seed, measured in a straight line, and the boundaries come out fairly smooth [[c08a:36-52 t:125-126 t:130]].

## voronoi.membership.manhattan
The same with Manhattan distance, the blocks across plus the blocks down: the boundaries turn sharp-edged [[c08a:57-73 t:131-133]].

## voronoi.membership.chebyshev
The same with Chebyshev distance, which the talk places somewhere between the other two [[c08a:77-93 t:134-135]].

## voronoi.walls
A tile inside the map’s outer ring becomes a boundary wall when the tile to its right or the tile below it belongs to another seed [[c08a:96-113 t:137-138]]. Seeds: {n}.

## voronoi.roads
Our own rule, for a use the talk describes without code: the boundaries become road and the cells solid, and a tile is road when its right, lower or lower-right neighbor belongs to another seed [[own]]. Seeds: {n}.

## noise.map
Fractal simplex noise is sampled once at every tile and drawn as brightness, from 0 to 1 [[c10:11-26]].

## noise.map#c11-overworld
Noise is sampled once at every tile and read as altitude: below 0 is water, from 0 up to 0.5 grass, and 0.5 or more mountain [[c11:20-44]].

## noise.first
The first noise on its own, with the demo’s base settings [[c13:23-29 c13:71-105]].

## noise.second
The second noise on its own: another seed, half the octaves and gain, one more of lacunarity and four times the frequency [[c13:31-37 c13:108-135]]. The talk calls it much more bumpy, and terrible as a continent [[t:169-171]].

## noise.zoomed-out
The whole noise at scale 1, cut into water, grass and mountain by altitude [[c12:31-47 c12:66-76]].

## noise.zoomed-out#c13-double-noise
The two noises mixed at scale 1, where the first counts in full and the second is taken away at a quarter of its strength [[c13:39-44 c13:139-149]].

## noise.scale
Every tile’s sample position is multiplied by a scale that shrinks a hundredth at a time, so a smaller and smaller part of the same noise fills the map [[c12:31-33 c12:77-91 t:167]]. The scale, in hundredths: {n}.

## noise.scale#c13-double-noise
As the scale shrinks, the first noise’s weight falls with it to a half and the second’s rises to a quarter, and both stay there from a scale of 0.5 down [[c13:39-44 c13:150-164 t:171-173]]. The scale, in hundredths: {n}.

## combine.input
Each input is built on a map of its own: in the talk’s demo, rooms in subdivided space, then a cellular automata map [[c09:41-79 c09:82-97 t:181-182]]. This is input {n}.

## combine.halves
The left half of the map is copied from the first input and the right half from the second, column for column, and nothing joins them [[c09:100-114 t:183-185]].

## prefab.show
The prefab on its own: two treasure tiles ringed by traps, inside a margin that is left as the map has it [[c14:86-102 c14:149-155 t:200-201]].

## prefab.placed
The prefab is stamped where it fits: at the center of a room big enough for it, or on a patch of open floor [[c14:104-124 c15:41-67 t:203-206 g]].

## prefab.placed#c09-combination
The prefab is stamped down the middle of the map, over the seam between the two halves [[c09:116-133 t:186-188]]. The talk’s is a line of fortifications; ours is a hall of our own design, because we don’t copy its layout [[own]].

## prefab.no-place
No place was found for the prefab. The talk’s loops end only when the prefab has been placed, so its code would still be looking [[c14:104-124 c15:41-67]]. Ours stops and records the seed as a failure [[own]].

## cull.center
The open tile nearest the center of the map is found [[c16:36-54 t:224 g]].

## cull.marked
A Dijkstra map from that tile gives every tile it can walk to a distance. Open tiles left without one can’t be reached, and are marked [[c16:62-73 t:225-229 g]]. Tiles marked: {n}.

## cull.removed
Open tiles that the open tile nearest the center can’t walk to are turned to wall [[c17:34-67 t:230 g]]. Tiles removed: {n}.

## start.desired
Where the start should be: in the talk’s demos, the middle of the left edge or the center of the map [[c17:70 c19:69 t:241-242]].

## start.placed
The start goes on the open tile nearest that point in a straight line [[c17:71-86 t:242-243]].

## exit.placed
The exit goes on the open tile nearest the middle of the right edge, and the route from the start to it is found [[c18:92-111 t:247-248 g]]. Steps on the shortest route: {n}.

## exit.placed#c19-ending-dijkstra
A Dijkstra map from the start gives every tile it can reach its walking distance, and the exit goes on the tile with the largest: the least accessible one [[c19:92-103 t:249-250]]. Steps on the shortest route: {n}.

## exit.no-path
The exit is placed, but no route reaches it from the start: without a cull, the two can land in separate caves [[own]].

## hot-path.marked
A second Dijkstra map starts from every tile of the route at once, so each tile’s value is its distance from the route. Tiles under a set distance, 10 in the demo, are the hot path [[c20:131-145 t:260-262]]. The radius: {n}.

## hot-path.culled
Tiles at the cull distance or farther from the route become wall. The talk’s demo culls at 15, wider than the 10 of its hot path [[c20:131-154 t:263-264 g]]. The cull distance: {n}.

## hot-rooms.marked
A room is on the route when a step of the route falls inside it. The demo highlights those rooms and grays the rest [[c21:129-156 t:267-269]]. Rooms on the route: {n}.
